  "articleBody": "Tearing out an old shower looks simple on TV, but rushing through demolition frequently damages hidden plumbing and framing. Here are key mistakes to avoid during the tear-out process.\n\n1. Skipping main water isolation: Never rely solely on individual shower valves, which can fail or leak under pressure. Shut off the main water supply and relieve system pressure before loosening any rough-in connections.\n2. Forcing wall panels over hidden pipes: Ripping down drywall or backer board with heavy sledges can puncture copper supply pipes or crack PVC drain stacks. Cut away wallboard methodically so you can see behind the studs.\n3. Ignoring potential asbestos materials in older homes: Disturbing older adhesives, joint compounds, or wallboard without testing risks hazardous fiber release. If materials are suspect, consult a licensed asbestos abatement contractor before tear-out.\n4. Leaving water-damaged framing in place: Rotted studs or soft subflooring under an old shower pan will rot further if tiled over. Inspect every stud, treat or replace compromised framing, and let the space dry completely.\n5. Capping rough plumbing incorrectly: Using mismatched temporary fittings or skipping pressure testing invites hidden wall leaks once the water turns back on. Always install verified, code-compliant caps.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\nProfessional trade support keeps tear-out clean, safe, and code-compliant. For dependable bathroom demolition, drywall repair, and tile preparation in Aurora and Denver, contact HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
